</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.jmb.2013.03.018</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23507311</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">37555154</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2024</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>30</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2813-1193</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>1</Volume><PubDate><Year>2022</Year></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Frontiers in neuroimaging</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Front Neuroimaging</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Extracting default mode network based on graph neural network for resting state fMRI study.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>963125</StartPage><MedlinePgn>963125</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">963125</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3389/fnimg.2022.963125</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI)-based study of functional connections in the brain has been highlighted by numerous human and animal studies recently, which have provided significant information to explain a wide range of pathological conditions and behavioral characteristics. In this paper, we propose the use of a graph neural network, a deep learning technique called graphSAGE, to investigate resting state fMRI (rs-fMRI) and extract the default mode network (DMN). Comparing typical methods such as seed-based correlation, independent component analysis, and dictionary learning, real data experiment results showed that the graphSAGE is more robust, reliable, and defines a clearer region of interests. In addition, graphSAGE requires fewer and more relaxed assumptions, and considers the single subject analysis and group subjects analysis simultaneously.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2022 Wang, Wu